---
import { type GameDefinition } from '@/games/registry';
import GameLayout from '@/layouts/GameLayout.astro';

import TechWords from './components/TechWords';

interface Props {
  game: GameDefinition;
}

const { game } = Astro.props;
---

<GameLayout game={game}>
  {/* Rendered in the browser only: the round is random and the lists load on demand */}
  <TechWords client:only="react">
    <p slot="fallback" class="grid min-h-[36rem] place-items-center text-muted">
      Loading the game...
    </p>
  </TechWords>
  <noscript>
    <p class="text-muted">
      This game needs JavaScript to run. Nothing is sent anywhere, it all happens in your browser.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div slot="rules">
    <p>
      A tech term is hidden. Guess it in <strong>six tries</strong>. Choose a length of 3, 4 or 5
      letters, and optionally a category.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Green with a check mark:</strong> the right letter in the right place.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Amber with a circle:</strong> the letter is in the term, but somewhere else. A
        letter that appears twice in your guess is only marked twice if the term has it twice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Grey with a cross:</strong> the letter is not in the term. It is greyed out on the
        keyboard, too.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Only terms from the list of the game are accepted as guesses. If you type something else, the
      row shakes and you do not lose a try. When the round ends, you see the term, its category and
      what it means. You can copy your result as a row of colored squares.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rounds are unlimited and nothing is saved. The link in a copied result opens the same puzzle
      for whoever you send it to. You can also add <code>?seed=anything</code> to the address to get
      the same term again.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="keys">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <kbd>A</kbd> to <kbd>Z</kbd> type a letter
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Backspace</kbd> deletes the last letter
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> hands in the guess, and starts a new round when the round is over
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves to the length, the category and the buttons
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>The on-screen keyboard works with a mouse and with a finger.</p>
  </div>

  <div slot="built">
    <p>
      The rules are small, pure TypeScript functions with unit tests: evaluating a guess (including
      repeated letters), the state of the keyboard, the round as a value that is never changed, and
      picking a term with a seeded random number generator. The user interface is a React component
      that only draws what the rules say.
    </p>
    <p>
      The terms and their definitions are written for this site. Each length is its own file that is
      only downloaded when you play that length. Tests check every term: letters only, the right
      length, no duplicates, a definition of at most 100 characters that does not give the term
      away. The animations are CSS only and stop when your system asks for reduced motion. Nothing
      is stored on your device.
    </p>
  </div>
</GameLayout>
